Found the bug: `_take_2d_multi_object` fills out with fill_value for masked positions, then unconditionally loops `out[i,j] = arr[u_, v]` even when u_ or v is -1 (which wraps to the last element). The loop should skip masked positions. Fix: in the inner loop, only assign if u_ != -1 and v != -1. But for object dtype when fill_value is None and no fill applied... fill_value can be None. In original cython path, positions with -1 are set to fill_value. Here when fill_value is not None we prefill and should skip masked indices in loop.

Fix in _take_2d_multi_object:

```python
for i in range(len(row_idx)):
    u_ = row_idx[i]
    if u_ == -1:
        continue
    for j in range(len(col_idx)):
        v = col_idx[j]
        if v == -1:
            continue
        out[i, j] = arr[u_, v]
```

But what if fill_value is None (fill_value None means no prefill)? When would fill_value be None with -1 indexers? In take_2d_multi default fill_value=np.nan; maybe_promote returns fill_value converted. Actually maybe_promote with None? fill_value None -> dtype object probably, so out would be object and prefill with None... Actually if fill_value is None, `if fill_value is not None` skips prefill, and masked positions remain uninitialized garbage. Previously, they'd get arr[last,last]. Which is the buggy behavior. Better to also write fill_value in loop for masked:

```python
for i in range(len(row_idx)):
    u_ = row_idx[i]
    for j in range(len(col_idx)):
        v = col_idx[j]
        if u_ == -1 or v == -1:
            out[i, j] = fill_value
        else:
            out[i, j] = arr[u_, v]
```

This handles None too, and the prefill becomes redundant but harmless. Also check GH issue number for whatsnew? Not strictly needed but convention. Add test? Tests are fine to add but edits to tests are discarded for grading; still fine. Keep it simple: fix take.py, add whatsnew entry? Optional. Check existing test file for take: pandas/tests/frame/methods/test_reindex.py maybe. Let me apply fix and run tests.

